const claims: Claim[] = [
  // ── Room 1: The Raj and its categories ──
  { id: 'c-1857', text: `The great revolt of 1857 began with a mutiny of sepoys at Meerut on 10 May, spread across north and central India, and drew in rulers, landlords and peasants; rebels at Delhi proclaimed the last Mughal emperor, Bahadur Shah Zafar, as their figurehead, and the British retook the city in September.`, when: '1857', where: 'Meerut, Delhi', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-commons-records'] },
  { id: 'c-1858-crown', text: `After 1857 the British Parliament ended the East India Company's rule: the Government of India Act 1858 transferred its powers to the Crown, and a royal proclamation of 1 November 1858 announced the change.`, when: '1858', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf'] },
  { id: 'c-divide-rule', text: `Historians disagree about how far British policy after 1857 deepened Hindu-Muslim divisions on purpose ("divide and rule") and how far it simply worked with divisions that already existed.`, when: '1858-1947', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-sarkar', 's-jalal'], note: `Some scholars stress colonial classification and administration as active makers of communal identity; others stress older religious and regional identities and the choices of Indian politicians. The sources consulted do not settle it, and this exhibit does not either.` },
  { id: 'c-census', text: `Colonial censuses (from 1871-72, and every ten years from 1881) counted the population by religion, caste and other categories, and those categories were used to allot jobs, seats and representation.`, when: '1871-1941', where: 'British India', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-cohn', 's-metcalf'] },
  { id: 'c-census-effect', text: `Scholars such as Bernard Cohn argue that counting people into fixed religious and caste boxes made those boxes more rigid than they had been; others reply that the census recorded identities Indians already held.`, confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-cohn', 's-pandey'], note: `This is an interpretive debate about cause and effect. The fact that the census counted by religion is established (c-census); whether it hardened communal identity is argued among historians.` },
  { id: 'c-inc-1885', text: `The Indian National Congress held its first meeting in Bombay in December 1885, as a body of educated Indians petitioning for a greater share in government.`, when: '1885-12', where: 'Bombay', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-commons-records'] },
  { id: 'c-bengal-1905', text: `Viceroy Lord Curzon partitioned the province of Bengal on 16 October 1905, creating a new province of East Bengal and Assam with a Muslim majority; the move set off a mass protest and swadeshi (boycott and home-industry) movement led largely by Hindu professionals in Calcutta, and it was annulled at the Delhi Durbar of December 1911.`, when: '1905-10-16', where: 'Bengal', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-sarkar', 's-metcalf', 's-commons-records'] },
  { id: 'c-bengal-motive', text: `Whether the 1905 partition of Bengal was mainly an administrative reform (the province was very large) or mainly a move to weaken Bengali nationalism is disputed.`, when: '1905', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-sarkar', 's-metcalf'], note: `Curzon argued administrative efficiency; Bengali critics saw a political design; many Muslim leaders in the east welcomed the new province. The evidence supports both motives being present, but historians weigh them differently.` },
  { id: 'c-ml-1906', text: `A Muslim deputation led by the Aga Khan met Viceroy Lord Minto at Simla on 1 October 1906 to ask for separate representation, and the All-India Muslim League was founded at Dhaka on 30 December 1906.`, when: '1906', where: 'Simla, Dhaka', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-commons-records'] },
  { id: 'c-electorates', text: `The Indian Councils Act of 1909 (the Morley-Minto reforms) gave Muslims separate electorates for the legislative councils: Muslim seats were filled by Muslim voters alone.`, when: '1909', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-sarkar'] },
  { id: 'c-lucknow', text: `At Lucknow in December 1916 Congress and the Muslim League agreed a joint scheme of constitutional reform, the Lucknow Pact, in which Congress accepted separate Muslim electorates; Muhammad Ali Jinnah, then a member of both bodies, was one of its architects.`, when: '1916-12', where: 'Lucknow', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-jalal'] },

  // ── Room 2: Demands and counter-demands ──
  { id: 'c-goi-1935', text: `The Government of India Act 1935 gave the provinces elected governments with real powers; the federal part of the Act, which would have brought the princely states in, never came into force.`, when: '1935', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-khan'] },
  { id: 'c-1937', text: `In the 1937 provincial elections Congress won absolute majorities in five of the eleven provinces and formed governments in most of the rest; the Muslim League did poorly even among Muslim voters, and disputes over power-sharing in the United Provinces left a lasting bitterness.`, when: '1937', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-jalal', 's-khan'], note: `The headline outcome is widely repeated; the exact seat counts and the weight of the United Provinces dispute were not checked against primary returns in this session.` },
  { id: 'c-lahore-text', text: `On 23 March 1940 the League's Lahore session took up a resolution, moved by A. K. Fazlul Huq, the Chief Minister of Bengal, declaring that "no constitutional plan would be workable in this country or acceptable to the Muslims" unless "the areas in which the Muslims are numerically in a majority as in the North Western and Eastern Zones of (British) India should be grouped to constitute 'independent states' in which the constituent units should be autonomous and sovereign."`, when: '1940-03-23', where: 'Lahore', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-lahore-res'] },
  { id: 'c-lahore-wording', text: `The operative paragraph speaks of "states" in the plural and does not use the word "Pakistan"; the label "Pakistan Resolution" came afterwards, partly from critics in the press.`, when: '1940', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-lahore-res', 's-jalal'], note: `Only the operative paragraph was read in this session; the whole text and the history of the name should be checked against a scholarly edition.` },
  { id: 'c-lahore-meaning', text: `Historians disagree about what the Lahore Resolution meant at the time: a demand for a fully separate sovereign state, a bargaining position for Muslim safeguards inside a loose union, or a deliberately open formula.`, when: '1940-1946', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-jalal', 's-khan', 's-talbot-singh'], note: `Ayesha Jalal argued that Jinnah used the demand mainly as a bargaining counter; other historians read it as a goal that hardened by the mid-1940s. The text itself is vague about the number of states and their relation to the centre.` },
  { id: 'c-cripps', text: `The Cripps Mission of March and April 1942 offered India dominion status after the Second World War, with provinces free to stay out of the new union; Congress and the Muslim League each rejected it, for opposite reasons.`, when: '1942-03', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-khan'] },
  { id: 'c-quit-india', text: `On 8 August 1942 the All-India Congress Committee in Bombay passed the "Quit India" resolution; Gandhi and the Congress leaders were arrested the next morning and most stayed in prison until 1944-45, while the League used the interval to extend its organisation in the provinces.`, when: '1942-08-08', where: 'Bombay', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-khan', 's-commons-records'] },
  { id: 'c-talks-1944', text: `Gandhi and Jinnah held talks at Jinnah's house in Bombay in September 1944 on a basis for agreement between Congress and the League; the talks ended without agreement.`, when: '1944-09', where: 'Bombay', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-jalal', 's-commons-records'], note: `The talks and their failure are standard in the literature; the exact dates and the proposals exchanged were not checked in this session.` },
  { id: 'c-1945-46', text: `In the elections of 1945-46 the Muslim League won all thirty Muslim seats in the Central Legislative Assembly and a large majority of Muslim seats in the provinces, while Congress swept the general seats; the results made it hard for either side to claim to speak for all of India.`, when: '1945-12/1946-03', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-metcalf', 's-jalal'], note: `The thirty-of-thirty central result is widely cited; the provincial percentage varies between books and was not verified against election returns here.` },
  { id: 'c-punjab-politics', text: `In Punjab the Unionist Party, which drew Muslim, Hindu and Sikh landlords together, had governed through the war; its Premier, Khizar Hayat Tiwana, resigned on 2 March 1947, and serious violence followed in March in Rawalpindi and neighbouring districts, where Sikh and Hindu villagers were the main victims.`, when: '1947-03', where: 'Punjab', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-talbot-singh', 's-ahmed'], note: `The sequence of resignation and violence is standard in the literature; the casualty figures for March 1947 are uncertain and are not used here.` },

  // ── Room 3: The Cabinet Mission to Direct Action ──
  { id: 'c-cabinet-mission', text: `The Cabinet Mission (Lord Pethick-Lawrence, Sir Stafford Cripps and A. V. Alexander) reached India in March 1946; its plan of 16 May rejected a fully separate Pakistan and proposed a loose union with three groups of provinces, and the League and Congress each accepted it in part, reading the grouping clauses differently.`, when: '1946-03/1946-06', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-banglapedia-cabinet', 's-khan', 's-metcalf'], note: `The outline is standard; the exact dates of acceptance and the intent of the "grouping" clauses were taken from an encyclopaedia excerpt and secondary books.` },
  { id: 'c-plan-collapse', text: `After Jawaharlal Nehru said on 10 July 1946 that Congress could modify the plan, the League withdrew its acceptance on 29-30 July and called for "Direct Action" from 16 August.`, when: '1946-07', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-banglapedia-cabinet', 's-wiki-dad', 's-khan'], note: `Historians disagree about how far Nehru's remark or the League's wider aims caused the collapse; the dates are secure, the blame is argued.` },
  { id: 'c-dad', text: `On Direct Action Day, 16 August 1946, a League hartal in Calcutta turned into about four days of killing between Hindus and Muslims; the number of dead is uncertain, with figures from about 4,000 (the figure given to Parliament) to 10,000 or more.`, when: '1946-08-16', where: 'Calcutta', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-dad', 's-khan', 's-chatterji'], note: `No reliable count exists. Historians also differ on responsibility: the League's provincial government under H. S. Suhrawardy, Hindu and Muslim crowds and organised groups, and the police all appear in the accounts. This exhibit gives the range only.` },
  { id: 'c-noakhali', text: `In October 1946 Hindu villagers in Noakhali and Tippera districts of East Bengal were attacked by Muslim crowds, with killing, forced conversion, abduction and arson; official figures are in the hundreds and some estimates near 5,000. Gandhi travelled through the district on foot from November 1946 to early 1947.`, when: '1946-10', where: 'Noakhali, Bengal', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-noakhali', 's-khan', 's-chatterji'], note: `The death toll is disputed between official records and later estimates, and accounts differ on how organised the attacks were. Gandhi's presence is well documented.` },
  { id: 'c-bihar', text: `From 24 October to early November 1946 Hindu crowds in Bihar attacked Muslims; the Congress provincial government's estimate was about 2,000 dead, the figure given to the British Parliament about 5,000, and Jinnah claimed 30,000.`, when: '1946-10/1946-11', where: 'Bihar', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-bihar', 's-khan'], note: `The figures come from interested parties and no reliable count exists. The scale is agreed to have been very serious; the number is not.` },
  { id: 'c-interim', text: `An interim government took office on 2 September 1946 with Jawaharlal Nehru as Vice-President of the Executive Council; the Muslim League stayed out at first and joined in October 1946, with Liaquat Ali Khan as Finance Member.`, when: '1946-09/1946-10', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-banglapedia-cabinet', 's-khan'] },

  // ── Room 4: Mountbatten and the plan ──
  { id: 'c-attlee', text: `On 20 February 1947 Prime Minister Clement Attlee announced that Britain would hand over power in India by June 1948, and that Lord Mountbatten would replace Lord Wavell as Viceroy.`, when: '1947-02-20', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-mountbatten-plan', 's-khan', 's-mansergh'] },
  { id: 'c-mountbatten', text: `Lord Louis Mountbatten was sworn in as the last Viceroy on 24 March 1947 and within weeks concluded that a united India could not be saved and that the transfer of power had to be quicker.`, when: '1947-03-24', where: 'New Delhi', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-talbot-singh'], note: `The swearing-in date is routine; the timing of his conclusion about unity is taken from the secondary literature and is bound up with the debate in c-date-reason.` },
  { id: 'c-3june', text: `On 2 to 3 June 1947 Mountbatten announced a plan that the provincial assemblies of Punjab and Bengal would vote on dividing their provinces, that referendums would be held in the North-West Frontier Province and the Sylhet district of Assam, and that power would pass to two successor states.`, when: '1947-06-03', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-nam', 's-khan', 's-wiki-mountbatten-plan'] },
  { id: 'c-date', text: `At a press conference on 4 June 1947 Mountbatten named 15 August 1947 for the transfer of power, ten months ahead of the June 1948 limit that Attlee had set.`, when: '1947-06-04', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-talbot-singh', 's-nam'] },
  { id: 'c-date-reason', text: `Historians disagree about whether bringing the date forward was a necessary act to stop British authority from collapsing amid rising violence, or a reckless haste that left no time to plan the division of the army, the civil service, the provinces and the assets.`, when: '1947', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-talbot-singh', 's-hajari'], note: `Defenders point to the evaporation of administrative control and the risk of civil war; critics point to a boundary drawn in five weeks and security forces split at the last minute. Both sides cite the same records.` },
  { id: 'c-date-tradition', text: `A popular story says Mountbatten chose 15 August because it was the second anniversary of Japan's surrender in 1945, and that he did so almost on the spur of the moment.`, when: '1947-06', confidence: 'tradition', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-hajari'], note: `The story rests largely on Mountbatten's own later recollections and on popular accounts; it was not verified against a contemporary record in this session. Some Indian astrologers also objected to the date, and Nehru's speech was set at midnight on the 14th.` },
  { id: 'c-assembly-votes', text: `In late June 1947 the legislators of the Hindu-majority and Muslim-majority sections of the Bengal and Punjab assemblies voted separately on whether to divide their provinces, and the votes went for partition; a last proposal for a united sovereign Bengal, backed by H. S. Suhrawardy and Sarat Chandra Bose, failed.`, when: '1947-06', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-chatterji', 's-khan', 's-talbot-singh'], note: `The outcome and the failure of the United Bengal scheme are standard; the exact vote counts were not checked.` },
  { id: 'c-referendums', text: `In the North-West Frontier Province's referendum of July 1947, 289,244 votes (99.02 per cent) were for Pakistan and 2,874 for India on a turnout of about 51 per cent, with the Khudai Khidmatgars and the provincial Congress government boycotting; in Sylhet, 239,619 votes were for East Bengal and 184,041 for staying in Assam.`, when: '1947-07', where: 'NWFP, Sylhet', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-nwfp', 's-banglapedia-sylhet'], note: `Figures are from encyclopaedia excerpts. The boycott means the NWFP result is read very differently by different historians, and the options offered (no independence or union with Afghanistan on the ballot) are themselves disputed.` },
  { id: 'c-act', text: `The Indian Independence Act 1947 received Royal Assent on 18 July 1947 and provided that "As from the fifteenth day of August, nineteen hundred and forty-seven, two independent Dominions shall be set up in India, to be known respectively as India and Pakistan."`, when: '1947-07-18', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-ind-act'] },
  { id: 'c-paramountcy', text: `Section 7 of the Act provided that the Crown's suzerainty over the Indian States lapsed on the appointed day, "and with it, all treaties and agreements" between the Crown and the rulers; the states were left to decide their own relation to the new Dominions.`, when: '1947', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-ind-act'] },
  { id: 'c-14-15', text: `Pakistan came into being on 14 August 1947, with Mountbatten addressing its Constituent Assembly in Karachi; India's Constituent Assembly met in Delhi at midnight and Nehru spoke the words "At the stroke of the midnight hour, when the world sleeps, India will awake to life and freedom."`, when: '1947-08-14/1947-08-15', where: 'Karachi, Delhi', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-tryst', 's-wiki-mountbatten-plan', 's-commons-records'] },

  // ── Room 5: The line ──
  { id: 'c-commission-members', text: `Cyril Radcliffe chaired both the Punjab and the Bengal Boundary Commissions; each was made up of four judges (two Muslim, two non-Muslim) with Radcliffe as chairman. The Punjab Commission's members were Justices Din Muhammad, Muhammad Munir, Mehr Chand Mahajan and Teja Singh.`, when: '1947-06/1947-07', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe', 's-chester'] },
  { id: 'c-terms', text: `The Punjab Commission's terms of reference, announced on 30 June 1947, were "to demarcate the boundaries of the two parts of the Punjab on the basis of ascertaining the contiguous majority areas of Muslims and non-Muslims. In doing so, it will also take into account other factors."`, when: '1947-06-30', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe'] },
  { id: 'c-radcliffe-india', text: `Radcliffe, a London barrister, had never been to India before he reached New Delhi on 8 July 1947, and he had about five weeks to settle the two lines.`, when: '1947-07-08', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-chester', 's-khan', 's-banglapedia-radcliffe'], note: `The date and the claim that he had not been to India before are repeated across the secondary literature; the exact arrival date was taken from a search excerpt.` },
  { id: 'c-no-sittings', text: `The Punjab Commission held public sittings at Lahore from 21 to 31 July 1947; Radcliffe, who was also chairing the Bengal hearings at the same time, did not attend them but studied the daily record. The four members could not agree, so Radcliffe gave his own decision.`, when: '1947-07-21/1947-07-31', where: 'Lahore, Simla', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe'] },
  { id: 'c-award-dates', text: `The Punjab award is dated 12 August 1947 and the Bengal award 13 August; both were published in the Gazette of India Extraordinary of 17 August, two days after the two states came into being, so on 15 August most people did not know on which side of the line their villages and towns lay.`, when: '1947-08-12/1947-08-17', where: 'New Delhi', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe', 's-chester'] },
  { id: 'c-delay-reason', text: `Mountbatten has been accused of delaying publication until after 15 August so that the British would not be blamed for the awards; his defenders say the leaders needed time to prepare.`, when: '1947-08', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-chester', 's-khan', 's-banglapedia-radcliffe'], note: `The date of publication is documented; the reason is argued from letters and memoirs and none of the sources consulted gives a decisive statement.` },
  { id: 'c-radcliffe-quote', text: `In the Punjab report Radcliffe wrote: "I am conscious that there are legitimate criticisms to be made of it: as there are, I think, of any other line that might be chosen."`, when: '1947-08-12', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe'] },
  { id: 'c-canals', text: `The Punjab report says that "the truly debatable ground" lay between the Beas and Sutlej rivers and the Ravi, and admits that the award could not keep the Upper Bari Doab canal system or the Mandi hydro-electric scheme under one administration; it urges the two states to find joint control, and notes that the Dipalpur canal takes off from the Ferozepore headworks.`, when: '1947-08-12', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe'] },
  { id: 'c-lahore-amritsar', text: `The report notes the claims of both sides to Lahore and Amritsar, "the stubborn geographical fact of the respective situations of Lahore and Amritsar"; the line left Lahore in West Punjab and Amritsar in East Punjab, a few dozen kilometres apart.`, when: '1947-08-12', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe'] },
  { id: 'c-gurdaspur', text: `Critics have argued that the award's handling of Gurdaspur district (with a narrow Muslim majority) and of the Ferozepore headworks favoured India and gave India road access to Kashmir; scholars disagree about whether Mountbatten or Indian leaders influenced the line and whether it was dictated by the "other factors" Radcliffe cited.`, when: '1947-08', where: 'Gurdaspur, Ferozepore', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-chester', 's-sarila', 's-radcliffe'], note: `Lucy Chester argues from the surviving record that claims of outside interference are not proved; other writers, notably Narendra Sarila, make stronger claims. The text of the award shows the line following tahsil boundaries; it does not show who spoke to Radcliffe.` },
  { id: 'c-bengal-line', text: `The Bengal award placed Calcutta in West Bengal; it gave the Chittagong Hill Tracts (largely non-Muslim) to East Pakistan, and exchanged territory so that Murshidabad, a Muslim-majority district, went to India and Khulna, with a very narrow Hindu majority, went to Pakistan.`, when: '1947-08-13', where: 'Bengal', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-chatterji', 's-banglapedia-radcliffe', 's-chester'], note: `These widely cited outcomes were not read in the gazette text, which describes the line only in the schedule; they should be checked against it and a map.` },
  { id: 'c-sylhet-award', text: `The Sylhet award divided the Assam district: it transferred to East Bengal the area west and north of a line along the River Kusiyara, kept Hailakandi in Assam, and said that "No other part of the Province of Assam shall be transferred."`, when: '1947-08-13', where: 'Sylhet', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe'] },
  { id: 'c-radcliffe-papers', text: `Radcliffe is said to have destroyed his papers before leaving India, which would explain why the surviving record of his reasoning is thin.`, when: '1947-08', confidence: 'tradition', sourceIds: ['s-chester', 's-hajari'], note: `The story is repeated in popular histories; it was not traced to a primary source in this session. The published awards, the commission's proceedings and the viceroy's records survive, so the surviving record is thin on private reasoning rather than absent.` },
  { id: 'c-line-effect', text: `The line cut through the Punjab's canal colonies, railways and market towns, splitting a Punjab that had been administered as a unit; the Radcliffe report itself concedes that any line would be open to criticism.`, when: '1947', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe', 's-chester'] },

  // ── Room 6: The princely states ──
  { id: 'c-states-number', text: `About 565 princely states, large and small, covered roughly two-fifths of the land of British India and about a quarter of its people; their rulers had treaties with the Crown, not with the Government of India.`, when: '1947', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-khan'], note: `The count and proportions vary between books (the number depends on how the smallest estates are counted); these are the usual round figures.` },
  { id: 'c-states-process', text: `Each ruler was asked to accede to India or Pakistan by signing an Instrument of Accession, normally limited to defence, external affairs and communications; geography and the wishes of the people were meant to be taken into account, and most rulers acceded by 15 August 1947.`, when: '1947', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-metcalf', 's-khan', 's-white-paper'], note: `The "wishes of the people" principle was stated by Mountbatten and the Indian leaders and applied unevenly; the exact formula is debated by historians.` },
  { id: 'c-junagadh', text: `Junagadh, on the Kathiawar coast, had a Muslim ruler (Nawab Mahabat Khan III) and a Hindu-majority population; its ruler signed an accession to Pakistan, announced on 15 September 1947, though India refused to accept it, took over the administration in November 1947 after the Nawab left for Karachi, and held a plebiscite on 20 February 1948 that favoured India overwhelmingly.`, when: '1947-09/1948-02', where: 'Junagadh', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-junagadh-ioa', 's-wiki-junagadh', 's-khan'], note: `Dates and the plebiscite are from encyclopaedia excerpts; the instrument itself is on Commons. The legitimacy of the process is disputed, see c-junagadh-law.` },
  { id: 'c-junagadh-law', text: `India and Pakistan still disagree about Junagadh: India holds that the accession was invalid and that the plebiscite settled the matter; Pakistan holds that the ruler's accession was valid and that India's occupation was unlawful.`, when: '1947-1948', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-junagadh', 's-khan', 's-bose-kashmir'], note: `Both governments' positions are matters of record and the legal question has not been decided by any body both accept.` },
  { id: 'c-hyderabad', text: `Hyderabad, the largest state, had a Muslim Nizam (Osman Ali Khan) over a Hindu-majority population and wanted to stay independent; it signed a standstill agreement with India on 29 November 1947, and Indian troops moved in during Operation Polo on 13 September 1948, after which the Nizam's forces surrendered within days.`, when: '1947-11-29/1948-09-18', where: 'Hyderabad', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-hyderabad', 's-khan', 's-commons-records'], note: `Dates are from encyclopaedia excerpts and widely repeated in the literature.` },
  { id: 'c-hyderabad-deaths', text: `The number of civilians who died in the violence after Operation Polo is disputed: an official Indian delegation led by Pandit Sundarlal estimated 27,000 to 40,000 (its report was not published until 2013), other writers give much higher figures, and the Indian government at the time gave far lower ones.`, when: '1948-09', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-hyderabad'], note: `Known here only from an encyclopaedia excerpt. A specialist in Hyderabad's history should confirm the report's findings and the range of estimates before this figure is published.` },
  { id: 'c-kashmir-ioa', text: `Maharaja Hari Singh of Jammu and Kashmir, a Hindu ruler of a Muslim-majority state, had delayed his decision; after tribesmen from the North-West Frontier entered the state in October 1947 he signed an Instrument of Accession to India dated 26 October, which Mountbatten accepted on 27 October, and Indian troops were flown into Srinagar.`, when: '1947-10-26/1947-10-27', where: 'Kashmir', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-kashmir-ioa', 's-bose-kashmir', 's-commons-records'], note: `The existence of the instrument and its dating are not disputed by India; some historians question the sequence of signing and the arrival of troops, as in c-kashmir-status.` },
  { id: 'c-kashmir-status', text: `The legal and moral standing of Kashmir's accession, the sequence of events in October 1947, and what the people of Kashmir wished are all contested between India, Pakistan and Kashmiri voices; a plebiscite called for by the UN Security Council (Resolution 47, April 1948) was never held.`, when: '1947-present', where: 'Kashmir', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-bose-kashmir', 's-wiki-kashmir-ioa', 's-khan'], note: `India treats the accession as complete and lawful; Pakistan treats it as obtained through deceit or duress and rejects Indian control; many Kashmiris reject both claims. This exhibit does not take a position and records the dispute only.` },
  { id: 'c-war-1948', text: `The first India-Pakistan war over Kashmir ran from October 1947 until a UN-brokered ceasefire took effect on 1 January 1949; a line agreed at Karachi in July 1949 became the basis of the Line of Control.`, when: '1947-10/1949-01', where: 'Kashmir', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-bose-kashmir', 's-wiki-kashmir-ioa'], note: `Summary dates from the standard literature and an encyclopaedia excerpt; the Karachi Agreement date was not checked.` },

  // ── Room 7: Migration and violence ──
  { id: 'c-displaced', text: `Between about 10 million and 20 million people left their homes and crossed the new borders in 1947 and the years after; the number depends on what is counted and when.`, when: '1947-1951', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-partition', 's-nam', 's-talbot-singh', 's-bharadwaj'], note: `The National Army Museum gives ten million, Talbot and Singh about 14 to 16 million, an encyclopaedia summary 12 to 20 million. The range reflects different definitions (the western border only, or Bengal as well; migration before and after 1947) and no reliable count was kept.` },
  { id: 'c-census-migrants', text: `The 1951 census recorded 7,226,000 Muslims who had moved to Pakistan and 7,249,000 Hindus and Sikhs who had moved to India, a rough balance of about 7.2 million in each direction.`, when: '1951', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-bharadwaj', 's-wiki-partition'], note: `Reported through a search excerpt of census figures. A census-based count catches only those who survived to be counted and who reported where they came from, so it is a floor for the displacement, not a ceiling.` },
  { id: 'c-deaths', text: `The number of people killed in the violence of 1946-48 is not known: estimates run from about 200,000 (Penderel Moon, a British official) to about 500,000 (the Indian judge G. D. Khosla) to about a million (Butalia) and as high as two million.`, when: '1946-1948', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-talbot-singh', 's-butalia', 's-nam', 's-wiki-partition'], note: `No census or registry counted the dead. Historians treat 200,000 to 2,000,000 as the range of published estimates and many prefer the middle (a few hundred thousand to about a million). Higher and lower numbers are used for political purposes, so the range is shown honestly here and not collapsed.` },
  { id: 'c-missing', text: `Census-based work by Bharadwaj, Khwaja and Mian estimated about 1.26 million "missing" Muslims from western India and about 0.84 million missing Hindus and Sikhs on the western border, that is, people expected in the 1951 counts who are not found.`, when: '1951', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-bharadwaj'], note: `Missing populations combine deaths, undercounting and migration that was not recorded; the authors present them as estimates, and this figure was seen only in a search excerpt.` },
  { id: 'c-punjab-peak', text: `The most intense violence was in Punjab in August and September 1947; a Punjab Boundary Force of about 55,000 soldiers was set up to keep order but could not stop it, and the army that served it was itself being divided between the two states.`, when: '1947-08/1947-09', where: 'Punjab', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-nam', 's-khan', 's-ahmed'] },
  { id: 'c-army-split', text: `The Indian Army was divided: about 260,000 soldiers, mostly Hindus and Sikhs, went to India and about 140,000, mostly Muslims, to Pakistan, with British officers remaining to help the transition.`, when: '1947', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-nam'] },
  { id: 'c-trains', text: `Trains carrying refugees were stopped and attacked at stations and on the line in Punjab, in both directions; the arrival of trains carrying many dead became part of the escalation.`, when: '1947-08/1947-09', where: 'Punjab', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-ahmed', 's-talbot-singh'], note: `Attacks on trains are well attested; individual incidents and their death counts are mostly known from memoirs, newspapers and later interviews, and are not given here.` },
  { id: 'c-who-did', text: `Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs were all both victims and perpetrators of violence in 1946-48, in different places and at different moments; historians disagree about how much of it was spontaneous and how much organised by militias, political networks and, in some accounts, rulers of princely states.`, when: '1946-1948', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-ahmed', 's-talbot-singh', 's-pandey'], note: `The "spontaneous versus organised" debate is unresolved: Ishtiaq Ahmed and others document organised violence in Punjab, while others stress local fear and rumour. Any account that places all guilt on one community is not supported by the sources consulted.` },
  { id: 'c-women', text: `Large numbers of women and girls were abducted, raped, forcibly converted or married off in 1947; a figure of about 75,000 abducted women is widely repeated, and official counts of recovered women were far lower.`, when: '1947-1956', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-menon-bhasin', 's-butalia', 's-khan'], note: `The 75,000 figure is an estimate drawn from official and unofficial counts, not a registry; some writers give higher numbers (to 100,000), and the official figures differ again. A specialist in women's history (Menon and Bhasin, Butalia) should confirm the sources before the number is used.` },
  { id: 'c-recovery', text: `India and Pakistan agreed on 6 December 1947 to recover abducted women and return them to their families; India's Abducted Persons (Recovery and Restoration) Act of 1949 backed the work, which ran into the mid-1950s, and critics point out that women's own wishes were often overridden and that some women who had built new lives did not want to return.`, when: '1947-12-06/1956', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-menon-bhasin', 's-butalia'], note: `The dates are widely cited; the treatment of individual women's choices is the subject of Menon and Bhasin's and Butalia's critique and is described here only in general terms.` },
  { id: 'c-honour', text: `Butalia and others document cases in which families killed their own women, or women took their own lives, rather than risk abduction or conversion, and the later memory of these acts as "martyrdom" is itself disputed.`, when: '1947', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-butalia', 's-menon-bhasin'], note: `The cases are recorded in interviews and community memory; whether they were free acts, acts of pressure or acts of despair, and whether to call them sacrifice or violence, are questions about which survivors, families and historians disagree.` },
  { id: 'c-kindness', text: `Alongside the violence, survivors and historians record many cases of neighbours, colleagues and strangers who hid, fed and escorted people of other communities to safety.`, when: '1947', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-butalia', 's-neh-bhalla'], note: `This is a recurring finding of the oral-history literature; it does not alter the scale of the violence and is not used to offset it.` },

  // ── Room 8: Gandhi, refugees and rebuilding ──
  { id: 'c-gandhi-calcutta', text: `On 15 August 1947 Gandhi did not join the celebrations but was in Calcutta, living in a house in a Muslim neighbourhood with H. S. Suhrawardy, and in September 1947, after fresh rioting, he undertook a fast that helped end it; he then moved to Delhi.`, when: '1947-08/1947-09', where: 'Calcutta', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-guha', 's-wiki-gandhi-assassination'], note: `Dates and places are from the standard biographies; the exact dates of the Calcutta fast were not checked in this session.` },
  { id: 'c-gandhi-fast', text: `Gandhi began a fast on 13 January 1948 in Delhi, ending it after five days; its stated aims included peace for Delhi's Muslims and the release of the cash balance owed to Pakistan, a payment the Government of India had held back.`, when: '1948-01-13/1948-01-18', where: 'Delhi', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-gandhi-assassination', 's-khan'], note: `Sources emphasise different aims; some stress the payment, others the wider demand for communal peace. The fast's start and length are secure.` },
  { id: 'c-gandhi-killed', text: `Mohandas Gandhi was shot dead at Birla House in New Delhi on 30 January 1948 at about 5:17 pm by Nathuram Godse, a Hindu nationalist from Pune; a grenade had been thrown at Birla House on 20 January, and Godse and Narayan Apte were hanged on 15 November 1949.`, when: '1948-01-30', where: 'New Delhi', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-gandhi-assassination', 's-khan'] },
  { id: 'c-godse-motive', text: `At his trial Godse said that he acted because he blamed Gandhi for Partition and for favouring Muslims; the assassin's reasons, and the movements he came from, are disputed in India today.`, when: '1948-1949', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-gandhi-assassination', 's-khan'], note: `That Godse gave this justification is documented in the trial record as described in the literature; whether it reflects the facts of Gandhi's policy, and how far an organised movement stood behind him, are points of continuing argument.` },
  { id: 'c-camps', text: `Delhi's old fort, monuments and open grounds, and a large camp at Kurukshetra in East Punjab, were turned into refugee camps in the autumn of 1947, and similar camps were set up in Lahore, Karachi and elsewhere in Pakistan; the Indian government photographed the arrival of refugees at Delhi's railway station on 27 September 1947.`, when: '1947-09/1947-12', where: 'Delhi, Kurukshetra, Lahore, Karachi', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-zamindar', 's-commons-records'], note: `The camps are well documented; their size and conditions varied and are not given here.` },
  { id: 'c-delhi', text: `Delhi was transformed: its Muslim share of the population fell from about 33 per cent in 1941 to about 5 per cent in 1951 as roughly 330,000 Muslims left and about 500,000 Hindu and Sikh refugees arrived.`, when: '1941-1951', where: 'Delhi', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wire-delhi', 's-zamindar'], note: `Figures are from journalism citing census data and should be checked against the 1941 and 1951 census tables.` },
  { id: 'c-karachi', text: `Karachi became Pakistan's capital in August 1947, when it had about 450,000 people; by 1951 it had more than a million, and migrants from India made up 57 per cent of the city.`, when: '1947-1951', where: 'Karachi', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-karachi', 's-khan'], note: `Figures are from an encyclopaedia excerpt citing census data.` },
  { id: 'c-lahore-amritsar-city', text: `Lahore, a city in which Hindus and Sikhs had been a large minority, and Amritsar, with a substantial Muslim population, were largely emptied of those communities in 1947.`, when: '1947', where: 'Lahore, Amritsar', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-ahmed'], note: `The change is certain; exact proportions before and after were not checked here.` },
  { id: 'c-evacuee', text: `The property left behind by those who migrated, houses, shops, farms and workshops, was taken over by custodians under evacuee-property ordinances and laws in both states and handed to refugees; claims and counter-claims, and a "scramble for houses" in cities such as Delhi, shaped refugees' lives and neighbours' relations for years.`, when: '1947-1950s', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-zamindar', 's-khan'], note: `The scholarship on evacuee property (Zamindar) is rich; the exact titles and dates of each law were not checked in this session.` },
  { id: 'c-pact-1950', text: `On 8 April 1950 Nehru and Liaquat Ali Khan signed the Delhi Pact on the protection of minorities in each country, after a fresh wave of migration from East Bengal; the permit system for crossing the border, introduced in 1948, was followed by passports in the 1950s.`, when: '1950-04-08', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-zamindar', 's-khan'], note: `The pact's date is widely cited; the details of the permit and passport regimes were not checked.` },

  // ── Room 9: The long afterlife ──
  { id: 'c-indus', text: `The Partition split the Indus river system between the two states; the Radcliffe report itself warned of the need for joint control of canals, and the Indus Waters Treaty of 19 September 1960, brokered with the World Bank, divided the rivers between them.`, when: '1947-1960', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-radcliffe', 's-wiki-indus'] },
  { id: 'c-1965', text: `India and Pakistan fought a second war in August and September 1965, mainly over Kashmir, which ended in the Tashkent Declaration of January 1966.`, when: '1965', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-khan', 's-bose-kashmir'] },
  { id: 'c-1971', text: `In 1971 East Pakistan broke away to become Bangladesh after a military crackdown in March and a war in which India intervened in December; Pakistan's forces in the east surrendered at Dhaka on 16 December 1971.`, when: '1971', where: 'Dhaka', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-raghavan-1971', 's-commons-records'] },
  { id: 'c-1971-deaths', text: `The number of people killed in 1971 is hotly disputed: Bangladesh has said three million, while some independent scholars give figures in the hundreds of thousands.`, when: '1971', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-bose-dead', 's-raghavan-1971'], note: `Sarmila Bose's critique of the higher figures has itself been criticised; no reliable count exists and no estimate is endorsed here.` },
  { id: 'c-kargil', text: `In May to July 1999 India and Pakistan fought a limited conflict around Kargil in Kashmir, a few months after both had tested nuclear weapons.`, when: '1999', where: 'Kargil', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-bose-kashmir', 's-khan'] },
  { id: 'c-wagah', text: `Every evening since 1959 the two border forces have held a joint flag-lowering ceremony at Attari-Wagah on the Grand Trunk Road between Amritsar and Lahore, watched by crowds on both sides.`, when: '1959-present', where: 'Attari-Wagah', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wagah-press', 's-commons-records'], note: `The 1959 start is repeated in tourism and press pages; interpretations of the ceremony (rivalry, ritual cooperation, spectacle) differ.` },
  { id: 'c-samjhauta', text: `The Samjhauta Express, a train between Delhi, Attari and Lahore, began on 22 July 1976 after the Simla Agreement and has been suspended at times of tension.`, when: '1976', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-samjhauta'] },
  { id: 'c-kartarpur', text: `The Kartarpur Corridor, opened on 9 November 2019, links Dera Baba Nanak in Indian Punjab with Gurdwara Darbar Sahib in Kartarpur, Pakistan, about four kilometres across the border, where Guru Nanak is remembered as having settled; Indian Sikh pilgrims may visit without a visa.`, when: '2019-11-09', where: 'Dera Baba Nanak, Kartarpur', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-wiki-kartarpur', 's-commons-records'], note: `Dates and arrangements are from a search excerpt and press reports; the daily limit and the fee have changed.` },
  { id: 'c-museum', text: `The Partition Museum in the Town Hall at Amritsar, a people's museum built from donated objects and oral testimony, opened in 2017; its collection and the 1947 Partition Archive are largely the work of citizens, not governments.`, when: '2017', where: 'Amritsar', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-commons-records', 's-neh-bhalla'], note: `The museum's opening date and funding were not verified in a fetched source; the Commons file records the building as the museum. A specialist should confirm.` },
  { id: 'c-terminology', text: `Even the words are debated: "Partition", "independence", the "great divide", and in Urdu and Hindi/Punjabi taqsim and batwara, name the same events with different emphasis; historians also debate whether 1947 should be seen as one event or as a long process (Zamindar's "long partition") that continued through the 1950s and in Bengal until 1971.`, when: '1947-1971', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-zamindar', 's-khan', 's-pandey'], note: `The scholarly debate about the label is real; the Urdu and Hindi terms are given as commonly used words, not as the preference of any community.` },

  // ── Room 10: Memory in words and film ──
  { id: 'c-manto', text: `Saadat Hasan Manto, an Urdu short-story writer who left Bombay for Lahore in 1948, wrote some of the sharpest short fiction on 1947, including the collection Siyah Hashiye ("Black Margins", 1948) and the story "Toba Tek Singh" (1955), set in a Lahore asylum where inmates are to be exchanged between India and Pakistan.`, when: '1948-1955', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-manto-works', 's-butalia'], note: `Dates and titles are standard; no text of the stories is reproduced here because the English translations are in copyright and the originals were not read in this session.` },
  { id: 'c-faiz', text: `Faiz Ahmed Faiz, the Urdu poet, wrote "Subh-e-Azadi" in August 1947, a poem that welcomes freedom while questioning whether this was the dawn that had been hoped for.`, when: '1947-08', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-faiz-subh'], note: `The poem's theme is as widely described; its lines are not quoted because they are in copyright and were not read from a text in this session.` },
  { id: 'c-pritam', text: `Amrita Pritam, a Punjabi poet who left Lahore for Delhi, wrote "Ajj Aakhaan Waris Shah Nu" in the late 1940s, calling on the eighteenth-century poet Waris Shah to witness the suffering of Punjab's daughters; it is remembered in India and Pakistan.`, when: 'late 1940s', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-pritam'], note: `The exact date of composition and first publication was not verified; no lines are quoted.` },
  { id: 'c-khushwant', text: `Khushwant Singh's novel Train to Pakistan (1956) is set in a fictional Punjab border village in the summer of 1947, where a train of dead arrives from the other side.`, when: '1956', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-khushwant'] },
  { id: 'c-ghatak', text: `The Bengali filmmaker Ritwik Ghatak, who was himself from East Bengal, made films about refugee families in Calcutta, including Meghe Dhaka Tara (1960), Komal Gandhar (1961) and Subarnarekha (1962).`, when: '1960-1962', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-ghatak'] },
  { id: 'c-sahni', text: `Bhisham Sahni's Hindi novel Tamas (1974), set in a Punjab town in the days before the 1947 violence, was adapted for television in 1988.`, when: '1974', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-sahni'], note: `The date of the television version was not checked in this session.` },

  // ── Room 11: How we know ──
  { id: 'c-mansergh', text: `The official British record of the transfer of power is published in twelve volumes (Mansergh, 1970-83), including the 1948 Foreign Office boundary maps; it is the base on which most diplomatic histories rest, and it shows the viceroy's and the British government's view.`, when: '1970-1983', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-mansergh'] },
  { id: 'c-archive', text: `The 1947 Partition Archive, begun in 2010 by Guneeta Singh Bhalla in Berkeley, has collected more than 10,000 recorded interviews with survivors (more than 10,300 in 2022), many by trained volunteers called citizen historians.`, when: '2010-present', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-neh-bhalla', 's-1947-archive'] },
  { id: 'c-oral-limits', text: `Oral history of Partition has limits as well as gifts: the interviews were mostly made decades later, memories are shaped by later national stories and by what could be said in each community, and those who died in 1947 cannot be interviewed.`, when: '2010-present', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-butalia', 's-pandey', 's-neh-bhalla'], note: `A general methodological point drawn from Butalia and Pandey; the specific effect on any one account varies.` },
  { id: 'c-rumour', text: `Gyanendra Pandey has pointed out that, without verifiable figures, the historical discourse on the killings of 1947 "continues to bear the stamp of rumour".`, when: '2001', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-pandey', 's-talbot-singh'], note: `The words reach this exhibit through a secondary summary of Pandey; the phrase should be checked against Remembering Partition before it is printed as a direct quotation.` },
  { id: 'c-history-turn', text: `Since the 1990s, historians such as Butalia, Menon and Bhasin, and Pandey have studied the experiences of ordinary people, women, children, and low-caste and Dalit communities, adding to earlier histories centred on political negotiations.`, when: '1990s-present', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-butalia', 's-menon-bhasin', 's-pandey'], note: `The shift is widely described; the roles of Dalit communities in particular deserve fuller treatment than this exhibit gives, and a specialist reviewer should add it.` },

  // ── Room 12: Voices ──
  { id: 'c-jinnah-speech', text: `On 11 August 1947 Jinnah told Pakistan's Constituent Assembly: "You are free; you are free to go to your temples, you are free to go to your mosques or to any other place or worship [sic] in this State of Pakistan. You may belong to any religion or caste or creed -- that has nothing to do with the business of the State."`, when: '1947-08-11', where: 'Karachi', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-jinnah-1947'] },
  { id: 'c-jinnah-speech-debate', text: `Historians and politicians in Pakistan have argued ever since about what Jinnah meant on 11 August: a secular promise of equal citizenship, or a statement about fair treatment of minorities within an Islamic state.`, when: '1947-present', confidence: 'contested', sourceIds: ['s-jinnah-1947', 's-jalal', 's-talbot-singh'], note: `The words are not in dispute; their meaning, and the way the speech was handled by Pakistani authorities afterwards, are argued about.` },
  { id: 'c-nehru-speech', text: `In his speech at midnight Nehru said that "Some of those pains continue even now", while also calling the moment one of freedom, thus naming the violence that was already under way as the new state was born.`, when: '1947-08-14', where: 'New Delhi', confidence: 'established', sourceIds: ['s-tryst'] },
  { id: 'c-hardeep', text: `Hardeep Singh, a survivor interviewed for the 1947 Partition Archive and described in the article as having been a refugee three times, said in an interview as quoted by Bhalla (2022) that "It can happen here also."`, when: '2010s', confidence: 'probable', sourceIds: ['s-neh-bhalla'], note: `The phrase is taken from a fetched summary of the NEH article and should be checked against the printed article and the archive before use; one short phrase only is quoted.` },
];

